"A major scale has a pattern of seven steps: whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half. That is W W H W W W H. Start on C and you only need white keys: C D E F G A B C. The two half steps fall between the third and fourth notes, and between the seventh and eighth. In equal temperament, the notes of a major scale are 0, 200, 400, 500, 700, 900, 1100, and 1200 cents above the tonic.": "1bf2efdc65a2",
"A pentatonic scale has five notes in an octave.": "76943abbe66c",
"A scale is a ladder of notes from low to high.": "3315be8627f9",
"A scale is a series of notes arranged from low to high, from one note up to the same note an octave higher. The first note is called the tonic. The English word scale comes from the Latin scala, which means ladder, and that is a good picture: every note is a rung, and you climb from the tonic to the top.": "eb93387ec5d7",
"A scale is a series of notes arranged from low to high, from one note up to the same note an octave higher. The first note is called the tonic. The English word scale comes from the Latin scala, which means ladder, and that is a good picture: every note is a rung, and you climb from the tonic to the top. The distance between neighboring rungs comes in two sizes. A half step, also called a semitone, is the distance between two neighboring keys on a piano. A whole step is two half steps. In the equal temperament you met in lesson 1, each half step is a frequency ratio of 2 to the power of 1/12, so two half steps multiply the frequency by about 1.12. On the piano, E to F and B to C are half steps, because there is no black key between them. A major scale has a pattern of seven steps: whole, whole, half, whole, whole, whole, half. That is W W H W W W H. Start on C and you only need white keys: C D E F G A B C. The two half steps fall between the third and fourth notes, and between the seventh and eighth. In equal temperament, the notes of a major scale are 0, 200, 400, 500, 700, 900, 1100, and 1200 cents above the tonic. The pattern is what makes the scale, not the starting note. Start on D and keep W W H W W W H, and you must use F♯ and C♯. A sharp raises a note by a half step. A flat lowers it. A natural minor scale has a different pattern: W H W W H W W. Compared with a major scale from the same tonic, its third, sixth, and seventh notes are a half step lower. From A, it uses only white keys: A B C D E F G A. Many people hear major scales as brighter and minor scales as more serious or sad. Taiwan’s dictionary says a major key usually sounds brighter than a minor one. But research suggests this link is at least partly learned, and it also depends on tempo and context. So try it yourself: play the same tune in both, and describe what you hear in your own words. Not every scale has seven notes. A pentatonic scale has five notes in an octave. The major pentatonic scale uses the first, second, third, fifth, and sixth notes of a major scale: C D E G A. The five black keys on a piano also make a pentatonic scale. Pentatonic scales are found in many cultures. In Chinese music, the five notes are named gong, shang, jue, zhi, and yu, and each of them can be the tonic of its own mode.": "b46897490531",
